  "result": "There is a particular kind of quiet in a library when rain begins. People glance up from their books as though someone has softly knocked on the ceiling. Then they return to their pages, reassured that the world outside has agreed, for a little while, to make staying inside feel sensible.\n\nI like the idea that a library is a warehouse of unfinished encounters. Every closed book is waiting for someone whose life will make it mean something different. The same sentence can be a window at seventeen, a mirror at forty, and a door much later.\n\nEven the practical details have their own poetry: the softened corners of a favorite novel, the date stamped on a forgotten slip, the faint protest of a chair being pulled across the floor. Nothing here needs to be new to be useful. Wear can be evidence of welcome.\n\nOutside, rain gathers along the curb and carries small leaves toward an uncertain destination. Inside, someone learns how to repair a bicycle. Someone finds a poem that names a grief they thought was private. Someone simply rests in a chair without being expected to buy anything.\n\nThat last possibility matters. We build so many places around transactions that a room devoted to attention can feel quietly radical. Come in, it says. Be curious. Be tired. Know very little. Begin wherever you are. Beyond the windows, the weather will do what weather does. For now, there is a lamp, a table, and enough light to read.",
